#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
genesi-ai-image — local image generation and editing for Genesi AI Mode.

The AI Mode Monitor's Image page is a front end over this tool, the same split
as genesi-ai-turbo / genesi-find: everything that touches models, the disk or
the GPU lives here, where it can be run and tested from a terminal, and the
window only draws what this prints. With --json every subcommand prints one
JSON object per line on stdout; human text goes to stderr.

    genesi-ai-image status    [--json]
    genesi-ai-image catalog   [--json]
    genesi-ai-image pull      MODEL [--variant V] [--json]
    genesi-ai-image remove    MODEL [--variant V] [--json]
    genesi-ai-image generate  --model MODEL [--variant V] --prompt TEXT
                              [--mode generate|edit|img2img] [--ref IMAGE]
                              [--strength S] [--negative TEXT]
                              [--width W --height H] [--steps N] [--seed S]
                              [--mask MASK.png] [--protect-faces]
                              [--lora ID[:WEIGHT]]... [--turbo]
                              [--out PATH] [--keep-llm] [--json]
    genesi-ai-image upscale   --in IMAGE [--model UPSCALER] [--out PATH] [--json]
    genesi-ai-image import    FILE [--name NAME] [--json]
    genesi-ai-image optimize  MODEL [--type q8_0|q5_0|q4_0] [--json]
    genesi-ai-image server    status|stop [--json]
    genesi-ai-image lora      list|import FILE [--name N] [--trigger WORDS]
                              |remove ID [--json]
    genesi-ai-image gallery   [--limit N] [--json]

The engine is stable-diffusion.cpp (genesi-sd-cpp, or genesi-sd-cpp-cuda on
NVIDIA), the same ggml family as the llama.cpp behind Turbo. Weights are never
shipped: the user downloads a model from the catalog when they want one.

── Turbo ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Measured, not assumed (WSL, 2026-10-03): the first picture from a cold engine
spent 221 s of its run just READING weights; the next one, from the same
warm process, spent none. So Turbo is, first, keeping the model loaded: a
genesi-sd-server held by a small keeper process that shuts it down after a
few idle minutes so the GPU goes back to the chat. On top of that, per model:
a Lightning LoRA that cuts a 30-step model to 4 steps, or a step cache for the
models that run many steps; and on CUDA, flash attention and SageAttention,
which the upstream docs say help there and only there.

── The catalog ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

Every file carries its size and SHA-256, so a download that was cut or a file
that is not the one tested is caught before sd-cli loads it. Files are shared
by NAME across models and variants, downloaded once, and never deleted while
another downloaded model still needs them.

Variants are quantizations of the same weights: smaller is faster to load and
fits a smaller card, larger is closer to the original. The SDXL checkpoints
arrive as 7 GB fp16 files and are converted to GGUF Q8_0 on the user's own
machine right after the download (`sd-cli -M convert`), which halves what the
card has to hold.

Licences are shown, not filtered: several models are non-commercial, and the
"unfiltered" tag marks models trained without a content filter.
"""
